<article class="env-proposition" id="007E">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/007E">Proposition <span data-tag="007E">1.5.6.7</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a simplicial set. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The simplicial set $S$ is an $\infty $-category. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every inner anodyne map of simplicial sets $i: A \hookrightarrow B$ and every map $f_0: A \rightarrow S$, there exists a map $f: B \rightarrow S$ such that $f_0 = f \circ i$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implication $(2) \Rightarrow (1)$ is immediate (since every inner horn inclusion $\Lambda ^ n_ i \hookrightarrow \Delta ^ n$ is inner anodyne). Conversely, if $(1)$ is satisfied, then every inner horn inclusion $\Lambda ^ n_ i \hookrightarrow \Delta ^ n$ is weakly left orthogonal to the projection map $p: S \rightarrow \Delta ^0$. It then follows from Remark <a data-tag="006U" href="/tag/006U">1.5.4.15</a> that every inner anodyne map is weakly left orthogonal to $p$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
